A source function forms a named top-level declaration.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    greet |name String|:
        io.line([: Hello, [name]])
    ;

    double |value I32| -> I32:
        return value * 2
    ;
]

### Declaration shape

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    name |parameters|:
        body
    ;

    name |parameters| -> ReturnType:
        body
    ;

    name |parameters| -> !:
        body
    ;
]

- The function name comes first.
- Parameters are enclosed by `|...|`.
- `||` declares a function with no parameters.
- `->` introduces the return contract: ordinary success and error slots, or
  the whole Never contract `-> !`.
- Omit the arrow only when the function has no return slots at all.
- A function with only an error slot still writes `-> Error!`.
- `-> !` is mutually exclusive with every success, optional and error slot.
- `Void` is not written as a return type.
- `:` opens the body and `;` closes it. An inline body closes on the line containing its `:`. A multiline body closes with `;` alone on its own line.
- Function names use `regular_snake_case`.

Scope placement follows @../language-overview/#blocks-and-statements (Blocks and statements).

Functions with required success values must return or otherwise terminate on
every reachable path. Terminality is validated before HIR lowering.

The absence of an error slot does not make private numeric work infallible.
Private functions infer and propagate implicit numeric failure within the same
module without postfix `!` at each internal call. Exported callables without
an error slot must discharge it. See @#returns-and-multiple-values (Returns
and multiple values) and @../progress (Progress) for the contract and current
support.

Parameters and return slots use exact declared types. Body-local numeric
inference never changes a signature or public interface.
@../numbers/#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) owns the permitted receiving
contexts. Generic signature relationships follow the separate bounded rules
in @../generics (Generics).

### Const functions

A const function declares that every call is executed by the compiler and
never receives a runtime body.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    double #|value I32| -> I32:
        return value * 2
    ;

    label #= [: result [double(21)]]
]

- The `#` between the name and parameter list marks required compile-time
  evaluation.
- Every argument must be a compile-time constant.
- Const functions admit immutable ordinary-value parameters and one ordinary
  success slot in V1. Mutable references, fallible return channels, receiver
  methods and recursive dependency cycles are rejected.
- A call in an ordinary function is folded during compilation. A call that
  cannot be evaluated is a source error rather than a runtime fallback.
- Invalid const operations, unresolved runtime arguments and nonterminating
  dependency cycles report the source call site.
- A const function with no calls is still type-checked and checked for static
  eligibility, but its parameterised body does not execute.

Release builds additionally fold ordinary functions whose whole transitive
body proves const-safe and whose call arguments are known constants. This is
an optimisation only: a refused or budget-limited call remains a runtime call,
and required const calls still report errors. Release budget advice is a
`Note`, not a failure. It names the setting to raise for a configurable
limit, and asks for a simpler call when a fixed compiler safety limit is hit.

### Never return contract

A callable that never returns control normally declares one whole return
contract:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    fatal |message String| -> !:
        assert(false, message)
    ;

    serve || -> !:
        loop true:
            io.line("ready")
        ;
    ;
]

The bare `!` sits immediately after `->`. It is not a type, a suffix on a type
or a reserved keyword. It cannot mix with ordinary return slots:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- Invalid: Never mixed with a success slot
    bad || -> String, !:
    ;

    -- Invalid: Never mixed with an error slot
    bad || -> Error!, !:
    ;

    -- Invalid: Never followed by another slot
    bad || -> !, String:
    ;

    -- Invalid: Never combined with `!` error syntax
    bad || -> !!:
    ;
]

Keep `T!` as the final error slot of an ordinary return contract.

A `-> !` body is valid only when every reachable path after known-Bool
specialisation diverges. These exits do not satisfy Never:

- falling off the end of the body
- ordinary `return`, including a bare no-value return
- `return!`
- postfix `!` or `?` that returns an error or absence to the caller
- a branch that can complete normally
- a loop that can leave through a reachable `break` targeting that loop

Divergence is proved through false assertions, standalone Never calls,
normalised-true loops with no reachable self-targeting `break`, lexical
scopes, branches and exhaustive matches. A recursive `-> !` group cannot use
its own unvalidated contracts as proof. Collection and range loops stay
conservatively finite.

An unannotated user function whose specialised body always diverges is
rejected. Write `-> !` when never returning is the intended contract, or
write the ordinary return type the body is expected to produce later. An
explicitly typed ordinary function may currently diverge without becoming
Never to its callers. The compiler-owned entry is exempt.

Implicit built-in failure that would escape a `-> !` body is a closed-boundary
diagnostic. Handle it locally. A Never call has no error or optional value to
propagate.

The contract is exact on every callable surface: functions, receiver methods,
generics, traits, exports and binding-backed signatures. A body that happens
to diverge does not match `-> !`, and `-> !` does not satisfy an ordinary
return contract. Changing a public callable between ordinary returns and
Never is a public semantic change.

See @#calls-and-access (Calls and access) for standalone-only placement,
@#returns-and-multiple-values (Returns and multiple values) for the
distinction from no-value completion, and @../progress (Progress) for current
proof coverage.

### Generic functions

Declaration-site generic parameters appear after the function name.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    identity type A |value A| -> A:
        return value
    ;
]

Generic inference and bounds are documented by @../generics (Generics).

### Function values

Source functions are named declarations and cannot currently be used as
ordinary values. General closures, anonymous function values, generic function
values and higher-order polymorphism are outside scope. A narrower future
design for references to named, monomorphic, non-capturing functions remains an
open design question and has no current syntax or implementation commitment.
